namespace Multiplayer.Network.Sync
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Stable wire ids for every synced SURFACE on the unified <see cref="SurfaceRouter"/> — the
    /// surfaceId byte of the 0x67 envelope. Geoscape partition = 0xA0-0xBF (tactical rides 0x80-0x9F).
    /// Never reuse a retired id: a new sender on one would silently mis-route on an old peer.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// ─── RETIRED / RESERVED ids — permanent tombstones, do NOT reuse ─────────────────
    ///   • ids 1-30 / 60-79 (action surfaces) + 1-10 (state channels) — the old repo's per-kind
    ///     migration catalog (mirrored its SyncedActionIds/ChannelIds). Never emitted by this repo;
    ///     constants deleted 2026-07-22. The live partition starts at 0xA0.
    ///   • 0xA0 GeoWallet / 0xA1 GeoState — retired: wallet + per-channel state ride the generic
    ///     value rail 0xAC (GeoRail); no sender remains.
    ///   • 0xAA GeoResearch — retired 2026-07-26 (constant deleted same day): the bespoke research
    ///     side channel; research rides the 0xAC value rail + the 0xAB intent surface only.
    ///   • 0xB2 GeoScrapCart — retired 2026-07-26 (constant deleted same day): the shared scrap
    ///     cart's bespoke echo; the cart rides the generic value rail as mod root "M#cart".
    /// </remarks>
    public static class SurfaceIds
    {
        // ─── Tactical band 0x80-0x9F (arc A2 opens it; A1 needed none — entry rides the save transfer) ───
        public const byte TacTurn = 0x80;  // TURN CURSOR + MISSION END host→all: [seq:u32][op:u8][body], seq rides SurfaceSeq. op = turn(1, [factionDefGuid:string][turnNumber:i32] — the PRE-increment number both peers read at TacMission.OnNewTurn, since PlayTurnCrt is what does the +1) / end(2, [playerFactionState:u8] = the host's TacFactionState for the Player participant, the ONE authoritative outcome bit the native battle-summary branches on). ONE surface for both ops on purpose: a single seq stream is what makes it impossible for a stale turn message to overtake the game-over (they are strictly ordered against each other, which two surfaces could not guarantee). Host→all only — the client's answer to it is the 0x81 end-turn intent.
        public const byte TacTurnIntent = 0x81;  // END-TURN client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][factionDefGuid:string], op = endTurn(1)); nonce rides the peer-aware IntentDedup; host validates off its OWN turn cursor (TacticalTurnSync.Validate — the guid must BE the host's CurrentFaction, that faction must be player-controlled and actually playing) + executes NATIVELY (TacticalFaction.RequestEndTurn, the same call the host's own button makes), outcome returns to every peer as the 0x80 turn op. Intent-only surface.
        public const byte TacCommand = 0x82;  // PER-SOLDIER COMMAND host→all: [seq:u32][op:u8][body], seq rides SurfaceSeq. op = activate(1, [actorKey:i32][abilityDefGuid:string][hasTarget:u8]([TacticalAbilityTarget fieldmask+fields, TacAbilityTargetCodec] only when set)[fumbled:u8]) — the ORDER, mirrored so every peer PLAYS it natively for presentation (Arc-4's "ship the ORDER, never the pose"; the path is re-derived by each peer's own deterministic Pathfinder) / settle(2, [actorKey:i32][posX:f32][posY:f32][posZ:f32][ap:f32][wp:f32]) — the host-authoritative CLOSER after the action really ended on the host, because the one thing a peer CANNOT reproduce is the cost: Move_AbilityDef.ActionPointCost is 0 so TacticalAbility.ApplyCosts:931-944 charges nothing, and the real charge happens once at end of traversal (TacticalNavigationComponent.cs:800 ActionPoints.Subtract(distTravelled / DistanceToAPFactor)) against an interrupt-dependent distance (overwatch / StopReason.EnemySeen, snapped via MoveAbility.SnapPointAndDistance:48-69). Position rides with it because actors are dynamic nav obstacles (the mover disables its own at MoveAbility.cs:108) so the obstacle set — and hence the path — can differ per peer under simultaneous commands; the closer makes any such divergence cosmetic and self-correcting on arrival. ONE surface for both ops on purpose, exactly as 0x80 carries turn+end: a single seq stream is what makes it impossible for a settle to overtake its own activate. Host→all only; the client's answer to it is the 0x83 command intent. A5 CARRIES THE ENEMY ON THIS SAME OP AND TOOK NO NEW SURFACE: an enemy action is the same TacticalAbility.Activate an order is (every one of the twelve PhoenixPoint.Tactical.AI.Actions classes reaches TacticalAbility.ExecuteAndWait:1168, which is three lines over Activate:1078, and AI movement is the same MoveAbility a player click uses), so the host simply mirrors EVERY faction instead of only the player's. Two consequences ride here: the rider WHITELIST became a declared DROP list (the AI executes data-configured ability defs, which no whitelist can enumerate — TacticalCommandSync.LocalAbilities names the seven the game itself treats as ambient), and an AUTONOMOUS activation — one the engine raised rather than a peer ordering it, recognised by TacticalAbilityTarget.AttackType != Regular, i.e. exactly Overwatch/ReturnFire/ZoneControl/Synced — NEVER crosses in either direction, because every peer raises its own from the same replicated board and a mirrored copy would be a SECOND overwatch shot. A7 ADDS OP 3, select(3, [actorKey:i32][equipmentDefGuid:string — "" is "select nothing", which RagdollDieAbility:162 really does]) — SWITCHING A WEAPON IS NOT AN ABILITY, so A3a's prefix on TacticalAbility.Activate could never see it: the model funnel is EquipmentComponent.SetSelectedEquipment:242 (it raises the game's own EquipmentChangedEvent:266) and the tactical UI clicks it straight from UIStateCharacterSelected:748/751, UIStateShoot:854/862 and UIStateAbilitySelected:725/736. Un-relayed it was not merely cosmetic: the HOST validates every order with the game's GetDisabledState(), whose EquipmentNotSelected arm (TacticalAbility.GetDisabledStateInternal:435 -> isEquipmentOfSelectedGroup:481-499) tests the HOST's selection, so a client that switched locally had its next grenade or reload REJECTED. The seam is a non-blocking PREFIX (the same method is the game's own repair path — EquipmentComponent:56/102/118/272, RagdollDieAbility:162 — so blocking a client would leave it holding nothing the first time a weapon broke). The settle op also gained a trailing [forced:u8]: a settle the host sent BECAUSE it refused an order is applied immediately instead of waiting for the refused peer's stuck speculative ability to end. A settle is deliberately NOT applied the instant it lands — the receiving peer may still be playing the mirrored move, whose own nav would overwrite the snap silently, so it is held until that actor stops executing (TacticalCommandSync.ClientTick).
        public const byte TacResult = 0x84;  // RESOLVED-ATTACK RESULTS host→all (arc A3b): [seq:u32][op:u8][body], seq rides SurfaceSeq. op = damage(1, [actorKey:i32][slotName:string — "" is the actor itself, anything else is a body part named by IDamageReceiver.GetSlotName(); ItemSlotDef.SlotName is a def field so it is identical on every peer and TacticalActor.ValidateActor:1178-1186 makes the game itself assert it is unique per actor][DamageResult fieldmask+fields, DamageResultCodec][recvHp:f32][recvArmor:f32][actorHp:f32][ap:f32][wp:f32][dead:u8]) / resnap(2, [count:i32] then per actor [key:i32][hp:f32][ap:f32][wp:f32][dead:u8](A5: [lootCount:i32]([itemDefGuid:string][destroy:u8])* only when dead — the host's ARCHIVED corpse manifest, so a resnapshot-forced death drops what the host's did instead of keeping everything)[slotCount:i32]([slotName:string][hp:f32][armor:f32])*) / A4's ACTOR LIFECYCLE pair, on THIS surface and not a new one: spawn(3, [key:i32][componentSetDefGuid:string][factionDefGuid:string][participant:u8][pos xyz:f32][rot xyzw:f32][sourceDeployZoneName:string]) and death(4, [key:i32][lootCount:i32]([itemDefGuid:string][destroy:u8])*). WHY THEY RIDE 0x84: a spawn and a death have exactly this surface's shape — discrete, unrepeatable, unhealable by re-emit — so they inherit its contiguity check and its resnapshot recovery for free; and ONE seq stream is the only thing that stops a damage record from overtaking the spawn of the actor it names (the same argument that puts turn+end on 0x80 and activate+settle on 0x82). Two surfaces could not order against each other. THE SPAWN KEY IS HOST-ASSIGNED (TacticalActorKey.AssignHostKey) and every other peer adopts it verbatim: a DERIVED key is a function of a board both peers snapshotted and a mid-battle spawn was in nobody's snapshot, so no derived scheme can name it. The client rebuilds through the game's OWN TacticalDeployZone.SpawnActor:257 with a null instance-data template (the def IS a just-spawned actor's state; anything later is the damage/resnap ops' business), and its own deploy roll is gated off entirely (TacParticipantSpawn.DeployForTurn:397 draws SharedData.Random against live enemy positions the peers legitimately disagree about — aliens only move on the host). The death op's loot list is the host's pre-rolled corpse manifest (DieAbility.ShouldDestroyItem:187-200, one draw per item); when a death DOES come through damage the manifest rides on op 1 instead, because the mirror's death starts inside that very ApplyDamage and no later message could reach DropItems in time. Death itself is applied through the game's own trigger (Health.Set(0) -> OnHealthChange:616-622 -> Die), never a hand-rolled corpse; an evacuated actor is HIDDEN by the native ExitMissionAbility path riding the 0x82 command seam and is NEVER destroyed. WHY RESULTS AND NOT ROLLS: there is no shared or seedable generator anywhere in a battle — scatter (Weapon.cs:481/487/490), damage roll + shred (DamageEffectDef.cs:52/57), fumble (TacticalAbility.cs:1128) and jetjump all draw from the global unseeded UnityEngine.Random, and loot/spawns from SharedData.Random = new System.Random() (SharedData.cs:69); Random.InitState is used ONLY for map generation. Worse, a shot has NO hit/miss die at all — the outcome is a scattered trajectory plus a physics raycast, so two peers rolling independently would hit DIFFERENT TARGETS, not merely different numbers. THE STREAM IS DISCRETE, which is the one place this surface differs from every geoscape one: last-writer-wins only DEDUPS a re-delivery, and a genuine GAP is unrecoverable because there is no periodic re-emit to heal it — so the client checks seq CONTIGUITY, says so loudly, and asks the host for a full resnap through the 0x83 intent op resnap(2). Host→all only.
        public const byte TacAim = 0x85;  // COMMITTED MANUAL-AIM STANCE host→all (arc A8, law L81): [seq:u32][op:u8][actorKey:i32][targetKey:i32], op = aim(1), targetKey 0 = not aiming; seq rides SurfaceSeq (strictly-greater IS the last-writer-wins guard, and a re-delivery is idempotent). SHARED state, not a per-window local mode: whoever entered aim LAST owns that soldier's aim, and a peer switching to the soldier lands directly in aim mode off this table instead of opening a fresh local one. Its own surface and NOT an op on 0x82 because the two say different things: 0x82 carries ORDERS (discrete, unrepeatable, each one must be played exactly once), while this carries a STANDING VALUE whose whole correctness property is that the latest one wins and every earlier one is irrelevant — putting a standing value on the order stream would make a re-assert look like a second order. A8's FIRST attempt (3071859, ops 5/6 on 0x82/0x83) was reverted by 0252247 because its MECHANISM was wrong, not its wire: it fed a CoverPose to IdleAbility.ForceRefresh, whose consumption path IdleAbility.IdleAction:275-290 -> RefreshIdle:324 -> DoAimOrPeek:166 -> PathProcessorUtils.GetAimOrPeekPathPoints -> TacticalNav.ExecutePoints:185 is a NAV TRAVERSAL, so a relayed aim MOVED mirrored soldiers (a jetpacked one re-flew). Those two ops stay dead tombstones. What the mirror runs instead is the animator pair the GAME itself uses for a nav-free aim entry — PathProcessorUtils.SetAimParams:81-84 / SetNullNavParams:91-94, both bottoming out in SetParams:67-74, whose entire body is a loop of animator.SetInteger and which has no other callee — because TacticalActor.CurrentlyAiming:227-237 reads exactly those integers ("TravelType" == 7 = TravelType.Aim) and nothing else. The POSE never crosses (law 5): only the discrete pair does. Host→all only; the client's answer to it is the 0x86 aim intent.
        public const byte TacAimIntent = 0x86;  // COMMITTED MANUAL-AIM STANCE client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][actorKey:i32][targetKey:i32], op = setAim(1)); nonce rides the peer-aware IntentDedup. There is NO validator: an aim stance costs no AP, mutates no authoritative state and can be entered by any peer on any soldier (law 5's no-ownership rule), so the host's only job is to be the single writer — it writes the shared table and echoes on 0x85, dropping an announcement that already matches (the wire-side half of the two-gate dedup; the other gate is the per-frame SAMPLER in TacticalAimSync, which emits only when its live sample disagrees with the shared table). Intent-only surface.
        public const byte TacCommandIntent = 0x83;  // PER-SOLDIER COMMAND client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][actorKey:i32][abilityDefGuid:string][hasTarget:u8]([target fieldmask+fields] only when set), op = activate(1)) plus A7's selectEquipment(4, [actorKey:i32][equipmentDefGuid:string]) — the client half of the weapon-switch funnel above; plus A3b's resnap(2, no body — "I lost 0x84 records, resend every actor's state"); nonce rides the peer-aware IntentDedup. The ONE generic command op for the shared battle — no ownership model: any peer commands any soldier and the host arbitrates by ARRIVAL ORDER against a PURE validator (TacticalCommandSync.Validate — actor exists/alive, its faction is player-controlled and playing its turn, the ability exists and is a declared rider, the actor is not already executing an ability, the native disabled-state gate, and the AP/WP cost check). That AP/busy pair IS the arbiter for two peers commanding one soldier: the first spends, the second re-validates against post-first HOST state and is refused with IntentRail.Reject + nudge. No ownership table and no in-memory claim ledger (v1's died on every reload). The acting peer meanwhile plays its own click locally for latency-free presentation; the 0x82 settle is what makes that speculation safe. Intent-only surface.

        // ─── Geoscape action-relay envelope surfaces (spec 2026-07-02) — RETIRED tombstones ───
        // Were reserved for a single-surface generic intent framework (client intent → host outcome →
        // originator reject). The real framework landed 2026-07-23 as the IntentRail ENGINE instead:
        // families keep their own surface ids below (the surface byte IS the family discriminator —
        // SurfaceRouter already routes on it) and register op tables into IntentRail, which owns
        // nonce/dedup/dispatch. GeoOutcome/GeoReject never materialized on the wire: outcomes ride the
        // normal rail diff (0xAC) / the 0xAD order channel, rejects ride scoped DiffEngine
        // re-emits (IntentRail.Reject). Do not reuse these three ids.
        public const byte GeoIntent = 0xA2;   // retired: never emitted
        public const byte GeoOutcome = 0xA3;  // retired: never emitted
        public const byte GeoReject = 0xA4;   // retired: never emitted
        // ─── Old-repo per-kind geoscape mirrors — RETIRED tombstones ───
        // Ids reserved by the OLD repo's hand-sync codecs (GeoVehicleSnapshot / GeoVehicleTravelSnapshot /
        // GeoVehicleExploreSnapshot / HarvestFloatCodec / CrcProbeCodec). Never emitted by this repo — the
        // codecs were never quarried in. Their jobs ride the generic value rail 0xAC (vehicle placement /
        // travel metadata / exploration progress = covered value+order fields; harvest floats = client-side
        // presentation off its own native events). The old comments' "frozen client" premise is FALSE in
        // this repo: the client sim RUNS (clock ticks, law-4b gates skip the mutators). The CRC divergence
        // probe's job — the law-7 CRC-per-subtree backstop — landed 2026-07-29 as MsgCrcReport on the value
        // rail 0xAC itself (no side channel: DiffEngine.RootCrc / HandleCrcReport). Do not reuse these five ids.
        public const byte GeoVehiclePos = 0xA5;      // retired: never emitted
        public const byte GeoVehicleTravel = 0xA6;   // retired: never emitted
        public const byte GeoVehicleExplore = 0xA7;  // retired: never emitted
        public const byte GeoHarvestFloat = 0xA8;    // retired: never emitted
        public const byte GeoCrcProbe = 0xA9;        // retired: never emitted
        public const byte GeoResearchIntent = 0xAB;  // Research rail client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][factionGuid][researchId][pos:i32], op = start/cancel/front/up/down/insertAt); nonce rides the peer-aware IntentDedup; host validates + executes NATIVELY, outcome returns via the 0xAC value rail
        public const byte GeoRail = 0xAC;  // THE generic value rail (laws 5/6): host→all canonical metadata-guided diff deltas (inner = DiffEngine [MsgDelta:u8][seq:u32][kindDefs][entries], seq rides SurfaceSeq); client→host full-resend request on seq gap (inner = [MsgResyncRequest:u8], law 7 resync-on-gap); client→host per-root subtree CRC (inner = [MsgCrcReport:u8][rootKey][crc:u32][appliedSeq:u32], law 7 drift backstop — one root per second, host compares only QUIESCENT roots and answers with a scoped ForceReemit)
        public const byte GeoManufacture = 0xAD;  // Manufacturing queue rail (migration #3) host→all ORDER snapshot: [seq:u32][count:u16][(itemDefGuid, accumulatedPoints:float)×N]; the un-keyable _queue (dup defs → excluded from the generic rail) is carried by explicit order, seq rides SurfaceSeq
        public const byte GeoManufactureIntent = 0xAE;  // Manufacturing rail client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][itemDefGuid][index:i32], op = queue/cancel/front/up/down/scrap/scrapVehicle/quickProduce + cart ops cartAdd/cartRemove/cartScrap ([defGuid][isVehicle], cartScrap body unused); for scrap the index slot carries the item COUNT); nonce rides IntentDedup; host validates (def-at-index still matches, or storage has count) + executes NATIVELY, queue outcome returns via 0xAD, scrap/buy outcome via the storage + wallet 0xAC value rail, cart outcome via mod root "M#cart" on 0xAC. (Equip-family ops moved to 0xB3 GeoEquipIntent 2026-07-26 — the surface byte IS the family.)
        public const byte GeoPersonnelIntent = 0xAF;  // Personnel progression rail client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][charId:i32][body], op = spendStats(str,will,speed INCREMENTS — the module's own numbers are bonus-inflated display values, only their delta is model-level) / buyAbility(trackSource,slotLevel,buttonLevel,abilityGuid) / secondSpec(specGuid) / reassign(dstRef) / skillReset(no body — free respec, host's own _hasSkillReset gates it) / hire(siteRef,vehicleRef — haven recruit, host re-derives cost/recruit) / fire(charId — KillCharacter Dismissed + vehicle scrap tail) / hireNaked(name,templateGuid,level,siteRef — content key, descriptors have no id) / tftvRedeploy(siteRef) / tftvTrainDeploy(early:bool) / tftvPromote(siteRef,specGuid — civilian→operative creation, host runs TFTV's own method)); nonce rides IntentDedup; host validates + re-derives the SP/mutagen cost from its OWN numbers + executes NATIVELY (ModifyBaseStat / LearnAbility / AddAbility / AddSecondaryClass / ResetCharacterProgression / the TFTV statics), outcome returns via the generic value rail 0xAC. Intent-only surface — there is no host→all personnel message.
        public const byte GeoTimeIntent = 0xB0;  // Geoscape time-control client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][val:u8], op = pause(val 0/1) / speed(val = TimeControlModule preset index — mod parity makes the index the stable id)); nonce rides IntentDedup; host applies NATIVELY (GeoscapeView.SetGamePauseState / UIModuleTimeControl.SelectTimePreset), outcome returns to all via root "T" Paused/Scale leaves + the "TA" TimeAnchor on 0xAC (same-frame: the setters raise EffectiveScaleChangedEvent → DiffEngine change-driven flush). Intent-only surface — there is no host→all time message.
        public const byte GeoBaseIntent = 0xB1;  // Base-construction client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][siteId:i32][body], op = build(facilityDefGuid,posX:i32,posY:i32) / demolish(facilityId:u32 — the UI scrap/cancel-construction path, host refunds natively) / repair(facilityId:u32) / togglePower(facilityId:u32 — toggle only, host re-derives the target value from its OWN IsPowered + Stats.RemainingPower)); nonce rides IntentDedup; host validates (CanBuildFacility + CanPlaceFacility / CannotDemolish / CanRepairFacility / PowerCost + RemainingPower) + executes NATIVELY (GeoPhoenixBase.ConstructFacility/RemoveFacility(scrap:true)/RepairFacility, GeoPhoenixFacility.SetPowered), outcome returns via the 0xAC value rail (facility values keyed by FacilityId) + the structural create/destroy applier. Intent-only surface.
        public const byte GeoEquipIntent = 0xB3;  // Soldier-equip rail client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][body], EquipSync owns capture+validation+native replay; op bytes kept from their 0xAE tenure — never renumber a live op: setItems=8([charId][flags][slot triples] — per-gesture loadout commit), augment=10([defGuid][charId] — augment install, bought via Wallet.Take), scrapEquipped=12([charId][list][defGuid][count][charges][amount] — per-INSTANCE scrap of an equipped item)); nonce rides IntentDedup; outcome returns via the 0xAC value rail (GeoCharacter EntityLists + storage GeoItemDict + wallet). Split off 0xAE 2026-07-26 (the surface byte IS the family). Intent-only surface.
        public const byte GeoEventIntent = 0xB4;  // Geoscape EVENT-WINDOW client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][eventId:string][choiceIndex:i32], op = answer(1)); choiceIndex indexes GeoscapeEventData.Choices — def order, mod parity makes the index the stable id and CompleteEvent itself works in indices (GeoscapeEvent.cs:97) — with -1 = the native "no choice" resolution (UIModuleSiteEncounters.cs:562-566); nonce rides IntentDedup; the host arbitrates FIRST-CHOICE-WINS off the REPLICATED ledger (GeoscapeEventRecord.State must still be Triggered), pays the choice cost from its own wallet and runs GeoscapeEvent.CompleteEvent NATIVELY; outcome returns via the record's own leaves + every reward-touched root on the 0xAC value rail. Intent-only surface. (The vehicle family drafted 0xB4 for itself in docs/rca-geoscape-coverage.md gap A2 before the event family took it; it moved to 0xB5 as that note prescribed.)
        public const byte GeoEventRaise = 0xB6;  // Geoscape EVENT-WINDOW host→all RAISE — a PRESENTATION payload, not domain state: [seq:u32][eventId:string][siteRef:string][vehicleRef:string][title:string][narrative:string], seq rides SurfaceSeq (a re-delivered raise is a SECOND window, so the strictly-greater guard is what makes the surface idempotent). Refs are ordinary rail root refs (IdentityResolver.RootRef/Resolve — "S#<siteId>" / "V#<id>@<ownerFactionGuid>"), "" = the host's own context had none. This exists because GeoscapeEventRecord carries NO site and NO context (only id/timestamps/state/_selectedChoice/_triggerCount) while every [HavenName]/[AircraftName] token dereferences GeoscapeEventContext.Site/.Vehicle UNGUARDED — so a window can only be rebuilt from what the host held AT RAISE TIME, never from the mirrored record. Texts are the host's resolved Title.Localize() / Description.Last().GetText(context); the client prefers its OWN def wherever that def resolves non-empty (player's locale, no shared-def mutation) and stamps the wire string only where the local key is empty — the TFTV runtime-narrative case (VoidOmen_{0..19}). Host→all only; the answer travels back on 0xB4, and the record's own 0xAC leaves are the dismiss signal (no dismiss message exists).
        public const byte GeoModalRaise = 0xB7;  // Geoscape MODAL-WINDOW host→all RAISE — a PRESENTATION payload, not domain state: [seq:u32][modalType:i32][shape:u8][ref:string][n:u16][key:string × n][num:i32][priority:i32], seq rides SurfaceSeq (a re-delivered raise is a SECOND window, so the strictly-greater guard is what makes the surface idempotent). `shape` describes the host's `object modalData` by its RUNTIME type, never by a static ModalType→class table (the game's own mapping ends in a fallback chain, GetMissionBriefModal:1723): None (no data) / ResearchComplete (ref = the research's faction root, keys[0] = ResearchID, num = SwitchToResearchState) / DiplomacyReward (ref = Faction, keys = the shared ResearchIDs, num = DiplomacyShareLevel). Refs are ordinary rail root refs (IdentityResolver.RootRef/Resolve); the client re-fetches LIVE elements from its own mirrored containers, so NO text and NO def state rides the wire and each peer renders in its own locale. `priority` is the host's own queue priority, verbatim (raisers really differ: research 99, diplomacy 100, base-activated 0). Host→all only: the client's copy is built with a NULL DialogCallback and is never marked Persistent, so no button on it can run game logic and a reload cannot restore the game's own authoritative callback (UIStateGeoModal.RestoreContext:36) — hence there is no answer surface and no dismiss message, each peer closing its own copy. Which ModalTypes ride here is declared, totally, in GeoWindowCoverage.DeclaredModals (RailCheck L49).
        public const byte GeoMissionIntent = 0xB8;  // MISSION-LAUNCH client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][siteRef:string][n:u16][charRef:string × n], op = launch(1)). The wire carries IDENTITY ONLY — the mission is read off the HOST's own `site.ActiveMission` (a client's GeoMission is a structural mirror, and a wire reference could name one the host already cancelled) and the squad is rebuilt from the host's OWN GeoCharacter instances, since GeoSquad.Units holds live refs. Captured block-first at the ONE model funnel GeoMission.Launch(GeoSquad):226, which every route into a battle reaches (UIStateRosterDeployment.DeploySquad:331, GeoscapeView.LaunchMission:1043 incl. its skip-deployment arm, HavenFacilityController:149, HavenInteractionController:226, UIModuleSiteEncounters:612, StealAircraftAbility:93) — so the client never runs PrepareTacticalGame, never stamps GlobalTime and never rolls GenerateMissionThreatLevel. Host validates off REPLICATED state (MissionSync.Validate — site resolves, ActiveMission runnable, every chosen soldier resolves and is on the shared player faction, one standalone actor present, OccupingSpace sum within MissionDef.MaxPlayerUnits, at most one vehicle/mutog) + runs mission.Launch NATIVELY; the OUTCOME is not a delta at all — it is the native save transfer that pulls every peer into the battle (law 1, TacticalEntry + SaveTransferCoordinator). The CANCEL half of the same screen takes a GATE instead of an op (MissionCancelGate): backing out is navigation, and one peer must not delete a mission for everyone. Intent-only surface.
        public const byte GeoWindowIntent = 0xB9;  // WINDOW-QUEUE client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][stateTypeName:string][modalType:i32][result:u8], op = advance(1); modalType = -1 when the named state is not a UIStateGeoModal, result = 255 when it carries no answer). PEER AUTONOMY for the geoscape window queue (user mandate: an AFK host must not stop the game — "if one player is left, they can play for everyone"). GeoscapeViewSwitchQuery.ProcessQueriedStateSwitch:58-63 dequeues ONLY while _currentStateSwitchRequest == null and the sole writer that clears it is FinishCurrentStateSwitch:116 = a HOST CLICK, while every queued window sets PauseGame=true — so ONE un-dismissed window on an idle host freezes the shared clock AND wedges its whole queue for the rest of the session. The host-side handler funnels through the game's OWN chokepoint at two depths: a UIStateGeoModal is answered with UIStateGeoModal.FinishDialog(result):82, which clears the queue slot and THEN invokes the host's own DialogCallback over the host's own modalData (so ModalResultCallback:799 runs natively and host-side — brief Confirm→LaunchMission:1043 / Cancel→mission.Cancel, FactionSoldierJoin→reward.Apply, InterceptionBrief→InterceptionBriefCallback); anything else (UIStateAssetDeployment:66/:79, UIStateGeoCutscene:92, UIStateGeoscapeTutorial:37, UIStateReplenish:64/:69) is dismissed with GeoscapeView.FinishQueriedState():2164. ONE op for all of them. The wire carries WHICH ANSWER and an IDENTITY, never state and never a callback (law 3). THE IDENTITY IS THE SAFETY PROPERTY: the host advances only when its OWN current window matches the named (stateTypeName, modalType) pair — the same two axes GeoWindowCoverage declares on and 0xB7 already ships, stable across peers by law 10 — or a peer closing its own tutorial would Confirm a mission brief on the host. A mismatch is the ORDINARY case (every peer closes windows of its own all game long) so it is LOGGED, never IntentRail.Reject-ed: a reject's forced re-emit exists to pull back a client whose screen ran ahead of host state, and closing one's own window ran ahead of nothing. First-to-answer-wins, the rail's standing arbitration (law 5). Client capture = a PREFIX pair (UIStateGeoModal.FinishDialog records the ModalResult, GeoscapeView.FinishQueriedState sends) — non-blocking, because closing this peer's own window is presentation and mutates no state. Intent-only surface; there is no host→all window message, and the RAISE half for brief/soldier-join/interception/asset-deployment/cutscene is still owed (GeoWindowCoverage declares each).
        public const byte GeoCutsceneRaise = 0xBA;  // GEOSCAPE CUTSCENE host→all RAISE — a PRESENTATION payload, not domain state: [seq:u32][cutsceneDefGuid:string][priority:i32], seq rides SurfaceSeq (a re-delivered raise is a SECOND cinematic, so the strictly-greater guard is what makes the surface idempotent). This is the RAISE half GeoWindowCoverage declared owed for UIStateGeoCutscene: a story cinematic fired on the host and appeared on NO other peer at all, not even in their window history, because every raiser reaches GeoscapeView directly and nothing on the wire named it. The video is REPLAYED, never shipped — each peer runs its OWN GeoscapeView.ToCutsceneState:672 off the def guid (law 2) and the host's own queue priority, so it plays from that peer's own assets and its queue orders it exactly as the host's did; no playback state and no callback crosses. ToCutsceneState is the ONE funnel every geoscape cinematic reaches (research TriggerCutscene GeoscapeView:2114, GeoFactionReward.Cinematic:264 = the GeoEventChoiceOutcome field, GeoMarketplace:165/:175, the campaign intro GeoLevelController:743, the console cheat :2186). ToGameOverState:662 is EXCLUDED BY CONSTRUCTION — it builds its own UIStateGeoCutscene with an EndFromGameOver callback that ENDS THE LEVEL (:666), which is a host outcome reaching every peer as the native teardown, so patching the funnel the two do NOT share keeps that carve-out mechanical instead of a runtime test. Host→all only: each peer closes its own copy, and there is no dismiss message (same contract as 0xB6/0xB7).
        public const byte GeoVehicleIntent = 0xB5;  // AIRCRAFT gesture rail client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][vehicleRef:string][body], vehicleRef = the "V#<id>@<ownerFactionGuid>" ROOT KEY — id and owner ride the key, never the payload; op = travelTo(1, [siteRef:string] — the DESTINATION only, the host re-derives the route on its OWN navigation graph and calls GeoVehicle.StartTravel(List<GeoSite>)) / setEquipment(2, [wN:u16][defGuid × N][mN:u16][defGuid × M], "" = empty slot — the WHOLE weapon+module slot list, because GeoVehicle.ReplaceEquipments is the only native funnel and there is no per-slot mount/unmount entry point; the host assigns its OWN instances vehicle-first out of the slots + GeoFaction.AircraftItemStorage, so no HP/ammo rides the wire) / exploreSite(3, no body beyond the vehicleRef — the site is that vehicle's own CurrentSite, read off the HOST's graph, and the host runs GeoVehicle.StartExploringCurrentSite natively; the client's own call is blocked, or it would run a per-vehicle Timing.Start against its unfrozen clock)); nonce rides IntentDedup; host validates off REPLICATED state (VehicleSync.Validate — owner is the Phoenix faction, CanRedirect, destination resolves and is not the idle current site, docked at a functioning Phoenix base, slot counts match) + executes NATIVELY, outcome returns via the vehicle's own leaves (DestinationSites/SurfacePos/SurfaceRot/Weapons/Modules) + the storage on the 0xAC value rail. CREW boarding is NOT on this surface: GeoVehicle.AddCharacter/RemoveCharacter are already captured as the 0xAF personnel op=4 reassign (one gesture, one mechanism). Intent-only surface.
    }
